// Part 28: sport, in depth. Five new categories for the areas that had none at
// all, plus expansions to the four that existed but were thin.
//
// Before this batch the collection had 25 anatomy atlases, 19 physiotherapy
// links, 30 nutrition databases and 19 health references, and nothing at all for
// tennis, sports science, strength training, muscles or sports medicine. The
// existing categories leaned on regulatory and reference data; what was missing
// was the applied and the physiological.
//
// Structure follows how the material actually relates. Muscles and Physiology
// are the mechanism, Sports science and Strength & Conditioning are the method,
// Sports nutrition and Sports medicine are the two ends of the same training
// cycle, and Tennis is the one sport taken seriously enough to warrant its own
// category rather than a few links scattered across others.
//
// Sources are open-access journals, textbook publishers' own free material,
// governing bodies, and encyclopedia entries for the reference surface. The
// paywalled sports-medicine journals are deliberately absent; the open
// alternatives are better for reading anyway.
